# System Core (required rules)

## Scope
MUST: Apply identically across the monorepo. Language and framework neutral where possible.

## Role and response
MUST: Understand the request, reflect context, and only provide actionable results.
MUST: For ambiguous requirements, pick one reasonable interpretation, state it, then proceed.
MUST: Structure responses (steps, code blocks, file:line references).

## Code quality
MUST: Naming must reveal intent. No magic numbers or strings — use constants or config.
MUST: Keep existing indentation, quotes, naming, and directory structure. New code must follow existing patterns.
MUST: Functions and classes have single responsibility. No repeated logic — extract to shared utils/components.
MUST: Minimize dependencies. Respect pnpm-lock.yaml; do not change versions arbitrarily.

## Web — RefineUI (required)
MUST: Follow React/TypeScript conventions. Monorepo packages: react, tokens, utilities, version.
MUST: Follow existing package exports and peer dependencies. New code must match existing patterns.
MUST: Consider a11y (semantics, ARIA, keyboard, focus), responsive layout. Keep component API consistent with @refineui/*.
MUST NOT: Ship secrets in client code or build output. Consider XSS, CORS, CSP. No PII in logs or errors.

## Security
MUST NOT: Put secrets, keys, or tokens in code, commits, or comments. Use env or a secret manager only.
MUST: Validate user and external input before use.
MUST NOT: Include secrets or PII in logs, errors, or responses.

## Errors and robustness
MUST: Always consider failure cases: network, files, API, bad input, timeouts.
MUST: Error messages must be specific enough to debug.

## Documentation and maintenance
MUST: Comments explain "why" only. Document public APIs.
MUST: Do not make breaking or behavior changes without checking call sites and compatibility.

## Collaboration and checks
MUST: Before submitting — style match, error handling, no secrets, tests/validation, existing behavior preserved.

## Exceptions
Only relax a rule when the user explicitly says so.